      <h2 id="why-a-correct-file-size-alone-still-gets-rejected">Why a Correct File Size Alone Still Gets Rejected</h2>

      <p>Most rejected uploads aren't actually a KB problem. A file can sit comfortably under the maximum size and still fail because the <strong>dimensions</strong> or <strong>aspect ratio</strong> are wrong.</p>

      <figure style="max-width: 420px;">
        <img src="/blog-photo-signature-crop.webp" alt="Diagram showing face coverage and crop differences between NEET, JEE, and GATE exam photo requirements" />
        <figcaption>Crop guides and face-coverage zones for a passport-style exam photo.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>GATE is the clearest example. IIT Madras's GOAPS portal doesn't just cap the photo at 5–600 KB — it requires a pixel resolution between 200&times;260 and 530&times;690, with an aspect ratio between 0.66 and 0.89. A photo that's technically 300 KB and well within the size limit will still bounce if it's the wrong shape.</p>

      <p>The fix is always the same two-step sequence:</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong><a href="/resize/">Resize</a></strong> to the exact dimensions or pixel range the exam specifies.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="/compress/">Compress</a></strong> the resized file down to the required KB range.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Compressing an oversized, wrong-shaped photo first just gives you a smaller version of the same rejected file.</p>

      <h2 id="the-myth-that-exam-photo-specs-dont-change">The Myth That Exam Photo Specs Don't Change</h2>

      <p>A common assumption: once you find an exam's photo spec, it's fixed for the year. It isn't.</p>

      <p>NTA revised the JEE Main 2026 upload limits mid-cycle — the photograph's maximum size dropped from 300 KB to 200 KB, and the signature's maximum rose from 50 KB to 100 KB. Candidates working from an earlier guide, or a cached blog post, were preparing files against numbers NTA had already replaced.</p>

      <p>RRB is the more extreme version of this. There's no single "RRB spec" — each CEN (Centralised Employment Notice) sets its own numbers for NTPC, Group D, ALP, and JE, and they don't always match each other even within the same recruitment year. A guide that quotes one CEN's numbers as universal is guessing.</p>

      <p><strong>Treat any exam photo spec as time-sensitive</strong>, not a fixed fact. Check the specification against the current information bulletin or notification before you upload, not just the exam name.</p>

