      <h2 id="neet-ug-photo-signature-requirements">NEET UG — Photo &amp; Signature Requirements</h2>

      <p>NEET UG's application photo must be <strong>3.5 &times; 4.5 cm</strong> (roughly 413&times;531 px at 300 DPI), in JPG or JPEG format, between <strong>10 KB and 200 KB</strong>. The background should be white or off-white, with the candidate's name and the date the photo was taken printed at the bottom — NTA rejects photos missing this text.</p>

      <p>The signature must be <strong>3.5 &times; 1.5 cm</strong>, JPG/JPEG, between <strong>4 KB and 30 KB</strong>. It has to be handwritten in running script, not typed and not in capital letters.</p>

      <p>NEET also asks for a separate <strong>4&times;6 inch postcard-size photo</strong> (10–200 KB) used for identity verification at the exam centre — this is different from the passport-style application photo, and using the same file for both is a common mistake.</p>

      <h3>Fix it in two steps</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Open <a href="/resize/">Resize Image</a> and set custom dimensions to 3.5 &times; 4.5 cm for the passport photo (or 4 &times; 6 inch for the postcard photo).</li>
        <li>Run the resized file through <a href="/compress/to-200kb/">Compress to 200KB</a> for the photo, or set a custom 4–30 KB target for the signature using <a href="/compress/">Compress Image</a>.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="jee-main-photo-signature-requirements">JEE Main — Photo &amp; Signature Requirements</h2>

      <p>JEE Main's current 2026 specification: photograph <strong>3.5 &times; 4.5 cm</strong>, JPG/JPEG, <strong>10 KB to 200 KB</strong> (reduced from 300 KB earlier in the cycle). The photo needs a plain white background with 80% face coverage and both ears visible, and it must be a recent colour photo — no filters, no polaroids, no collage crops.</p>

      <p>The signature must be <strong>3.5 &times; 1.5 cm</strong>, JPG/JPEG, <strong>10 KB to 100 KB</strong> (raised from 50 KB). Sign in blue or black ink on white paper; NTA checks legibility manually during scrutiny.</p>

      <p>Both files get printed on the admit card and scorecard, so clarity matters as much as compliance.</p>

      <h3>Fix it in two steps</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="/resize/">Resize Image</a> to 3.5 &times; 4.5 cm.</li>
        <li><a href="/compress/to-200kb/">Compress to 200KB</a> for the photo; use a custom 10–100 KB target in <a href="/compress/">Compress Image</a> for the signature.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="gate-photo-signature-requirements">GATE — Photo &amp; Signature Requirements</h2>

      <p>GATE, run by IIT Madras through the GOAPS portal, is the most technically strict of the four. The photograph must be <strong>3.5 &times; 4.5 cm</strong>, with a pixel resolution between <strong>200&times;260 and 530&times;690</strong>, an aspect ratio between <strong>0.66 and 0.89</strong>, and a file size between <strong>5 KB and 600 KB</strong>. Face coverage should sit at 60–70% of the frame — noticeably less than NEET or JEE Main's 80% requirement.</p>

      <p>The signature must fall between <strong>250&times;80 and 580&times;180 pixels</strong>, with a width-to-height aspect ratio of roughly <strong>2.75:1 to 3.75:1</strong>, and a file size of <strong>3 KB to 300 KB</strong>. Sign in black or dark blue ink.</p>

      <p>Because GOAPS validates both pixel dimensions and aspect ratio — not just KB — a photo that passes the size check can still fail if it's cropped to the wrong shape.</p>

      <h3>Fix it in two steps</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="/resize/">Resize Image</a> to custom pixel dimensions inside the 200&times;260–530&times;690 range (aim for the middle, e.g. 350&times;450, to stay safely inside the ratio window).</li>
        <li><a href="/compress/to-500kb/">Compress to 500KB</a> and fine-tune toward your target within the 5–600 KB range using <a href="/compress/">Compress Image</a>'s custom KB slider.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="rrb-photo-signature-requirements">RRB (NTPC, Group D, ALP, JE) — Photo &amp; Signature Requirements</h2>

      <p>RRB doesn't publish one universal photo spec the way NTA does for NEET and JEE Main. Each Railway Recruitment Board recruitment runs under its own <strong>CEN</strong> (Centralised Employment Notice), and different CENs — NTPC, Group D, ALP, JE — have specified different KB ranges in different recruitment cycles, sometimes even within the same year.</p>

      <p>Across recent NTPC and Group D notifications, the most commonly cited figures are a <strong>3.5 &times; 4.5 cm</strong> photograph between <strong>30 and 70 KB</strong>, and a signature between <strong>3.5 &times; 1.5 cm and 5 &times; 2 cm</strong>, roughly <strong>10 to 70 KB</strong>, in JPG format on a white background. Some notifications have specified narrower ranges (20–50 KB photo, 10–20 KB signature) — the gap is real, not a rounding error.</p>

      <p><strong>Before you upload:</strong> open the specific CEN notification PDF for the recruitment you're applying to and confirm the exact figures. Don't rely on a photo prepared for a different RRB exam or a different year's CEN.</p>

      <h3>Fix it in two steps</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="/resize/">Resize Image</a> to 3.5 &times; 4.5 cm as a starting point, adjusting if your CEN specifies otherwise.</li>
        <li>Use <a href="/compress/">Compress Image</a> with a custom KB target set to your specific CEN's range — the <a href="/compress/to-50kb/">Compress to 50KB</a> preset sits close to the middle of the commonly cited photo range.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>

      <h3>My exam isn't listed here — where do I find the spec?</h3>
      <p>Check the official information bulletin or notification PDF for your exam. The two-step fix (resize to exact dimensions, then compress to the KB range) works the same way regardless of which numbers you're targeting.</p>

      <h3>My file is inside the KB limit but the portal still rejected it</h3>
      <p>This is almost always a dimension or aspect-ratio problem, not a size problem — GATE's GOAPS portal in particular checks both. <a href="/resize/">Resize</a> to the exact pixel range first, then <a href="/compress/">compress</a>.</p>

      <h3>Can I reuse one photo across NEET, JEE Main, GATE, and RRB?</h3>
      <p>Not directly. The dimensions and aspect ratios differ enough (GATE's 60–70% face coverage vs. NEET/JEE's 80%, for example) that a photo cropped for one exam often fails another's check. Start from the same source photo, but resize and crop separately for each exam.</p>

